<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euripus Strait</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Euripus is the narrow strait between the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> and mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, only about 40 metres wide at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a>. It is famous for its strong tidal currents, which reverse direction several times a day and puzzled ancient writers. The first bridge across it was built in 411 BCE, and Chalcis grew rich from controlling the crossing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euripus-strait.jpg" alt="Euripus Strait" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Darwinek · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Euboea_island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Euripus Strait separates the Greek island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> on the mainland, and joins the North Euboean Gulf to the South Euboean Gulf. Its principal port is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> on Euboea, at the strait's narrowest point. Tidal flows are very weak in the Eastern Mediterranean, but the strait is an exception. The current reverses approximately four times a day and peaks at about 12 kilometres an hour, northwards or southwards, so that lesser vessels are often unable to sail against it. Near the moment of reversal vortices form and make passage more precarious.</p><p>The name comes from the Ancient Greek eúrīpos, meaning any strait or narrow sea where the flux and reflux is violent. The Swiss scholar François-Alphonse Forel advanced the understanding of the phenomenon through his study of seiches, but the problem was solved completely only by D. Eginitis, director of the Athens Observatory, who published his conclusions in 1929.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Euripus was closed by a dike of coarse sediment until about 6000 years ago, when an unrecorded earthquake opened it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> mentions it in his account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-artemisium">Battle of Artemisium</a> in 480 BCE, which implies that large fleets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> could sail through. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> reports that in 411 BCE the Euboeans closed the strait by mostly rebuilding the dike, so as to become part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> rather than an island subject to Athenian hegemony. Gaps had to be left for the tides, and the narrowness of the passage made the current much more intense. Only a single passage, wide enough for one ship, remained navigable. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> later wrote of a bridge two plethra long.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> reports that under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> there were two channels, the large original one and a narrow cleft to the east that could be crossed on a single plank. The fortress there was called Euripus, which became Egripos by 1204; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> who occupied it in 1205 altered the name to Nigriponte. Venetian records of 1408 and 1439 note a reef under the wooden bridge and erosion around its pilings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> wrote that the narrow channel was first opened enough for a galley in the late sixteenth century and was still barely wide enough in 1668.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Two road bridges cross the strait at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a>. The Old or Sliding Bridge lies at the narrowest point, where the strait is only 38 metres wide; a retractable bridge was built there in 1858, replaced by a rotating one in 1896, and the existing bridge dates from 1962 and was refurbished in 1998. The Euripus Bridge, a cable-suspended bridge with a span of about 215 metres, was built south of the town in 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Euripus Strait” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chalcis grew on the narrowest point of the <b>Euripus</b> channel between Euboea and mainland Greece.</blockquote><a href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a></li><li><blockquote>In 411 BCE, after a Spartan naval victory off Eretria, its cities revolted and formed a league, building a bridge across the <b>Euripus</b> to link Chalcis with the mainland.</blockquote><a href="/state/euboean-league">Euboean League</a></li><li><blockquote>A Venetian bailo and councillors governed from the fortified town on the <b>Euripus</b> strait, where a bridge linked the island to the mainland.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-negroponte">Venetian Negroponte</a></li></ul></section>
